@comment{
====================================================================
DOMAIN: The Late Ontology of Social Being and the Continuity Question
SEARCH_DATE: 2026-07-17
PAPERS_FOUND: 21 total (High: 12, Medium: 9, Low: 0)
SEARCH_SOURCES: SEP, PhilPapers, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex, CORE, CrossRef
====================================================================

DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
Lukács's final, unfinished project, Zur Ontologie des gesellschaftlichen Seins
(written 1960s, published posthumously from 1971), attempts to ground Marxism
in a general ontology of "social being" built outward from labor as the
"model" (Modell) of all teleological positing (teleologische Setzung).
Where History and Class Consciousness (HCC) had located the resolution of the
subject-object antinomy in the revolutionary self-consciousness of the
proletariat, the Ontology relocates the analysis at a more basic level: every
act of labor posits an end (teleology) that is realized only through
manipulation of an objectively existing causal nexus (causality), and it is
this labor-model, generalized and made "more social" (gesellschaftlicher) at
successively higher levels of complexity, that is supposed to explain the
genesis of value, ideology, alienation, and historical development without
recourse to a metaphysical world-spirit or a single collective revolutionary
subject-object. The reception history of this text is unusually contested.
The "Budapest School" -- Lukács's own students Fehér, Heller, Márkus, and
Vajda -- published a scathing internal critique in 1976 charging that the
manuscript oscillates between two incompatible ontologies (a dogmatic,
quasi-Engelsian "ontology of nature" and a genuinely dialectical "ontology of
social being"), a critique that, per Forczek's 2024 rebuttal captured in
Domain 1 of this review, has disproportionately shaped (and arguably
suppressed) the Ontology's later reception. Nicolas Tertulian, by contrast,
across several decades of essays, treats the Ontology as Lukács's most
original and underappreciated achievement, particularly in confrontation with
Heidegger and Nicolai Hartmann. A separate, largely English-language strand
(Joós, Browne, Duayer and Medeiros, Thompson's edited volume, Dorahy) asks
whether the Ontology's categorial apparatus is best read as a fulfillment,
correction, or quiet abandonment of the identical-subject-object claims of
HCC -- a debate this domain surveys directly, in contrast to Domain 1's
broader periodization question and Domain 3's treatment of totality and
subject-object in HCC itself.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain supplies the endpoint of the continuity-vs-rupture debate that
runs throughout the review: the Ontology's labor-teleology model is Lukács's
own final verdict on the philosophy-of-history problems posed by HCC, so any
overall assessment of his constructive theory of history must take a position
on how (or whether) the late work resolves, revises, or evades the
subject-object and teleology problems left open in the 1920s.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
No complete English translation of the Prolegomena zur Ontologie des
gesellschaftlichen Seins (the introductory volume, distinct from the three
translated substantive volumes on Hegel, Marx, and Labour) could be verified
via the search scripts, so it is not included as a primary source here;
English-language treatments of the Prolegomena specifically (as opposed to
the translated volumes) remain thin. Guido Oldrini's extensive Italian-language
work on Lukács was searched but yielded no items specifically addressing the
late Ontology (his located essays concern the young Lukács and the Second
International); this appears to be a genuine gap in the English/searchable
literature rather than an oversight. The "post festum-rationality" debate
(Forczek 2024) and the Ninos 2025 Hegel-Logic critique are treated in Domains
1 and 4 respectively and are not duplicated here, though both bear directly
on this domain's questions.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
Organize around three axes: (1) primary-text description of the
labor/teleology/causality model itself; (2) the Budapest School rupture
charge and its rebuttals (Tosel, Duayer and Medeiros, Dorahy); (3) applications
and extensions of the ontology beyond exegesis (Fuchs on digital labor,
Blanton on judgment, Vaisman and Fortes on late politics). Cross-reference
Domain 1's Forczek entry and Domain 4's Ninos entry when discussing the
Budapest School critique and the Hegel-Logic lineage of teleological positing.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Rupture/incompatibility (Budapest School): 1 paper (Fehér, Heller, Márkus,
  Vajda 1976) - the Ontology mixes two incompatible ontologies and regresses
  toward Soviet-style dialectical materialism.
- Fulfillment/deepening (Tertulian, Duayer and Medeiros, Tosel, Browne): 5
  papers - the Ontology is Lukács's most mature and philosophically serious
  achievement, continuous with but superior to HCC's unresolved antinomies.
- Applied/extension readings (Fuchs, Blanton, Vaisman and Fortes, Correa
  Castañeda): 4 papers - treat the categorial apparatus (teleological
  positing, labor-model) as a still-usable analytic tool independent of the
  continuity debate.
====================================================================
}

@book{lukacs1978ontologyhegel,
  author = {Lukács, Georg},
  title = {The Ontology of Social Being, Vol. 1: Hegel's False and his Genuine Ontology},
  year = {1978},
  publisher = {Merlin Press},
  address = {London},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: The first of three translated volumes of Lukács's late Ontology, this volume works through Hegel's Logic to distinguish a "false," idealist ontology (in which categories such as teleology are illegitimately extended to nature and history as a whole) from a "genuine" ontology Lukács finds latent in Hegel and which he proposes to extract and rework materialistically as the basis for a Marxist social ontology.
  RELEVANCE: Primary source establishing the Hegelian point of departure for the labor/teleology model surveyed throughout this domain; provides the direct textual basis against which Domain 4's Ninos and Menozzi articles on Lukács's use of Hegel's Logic must be read.
  POSITION: Primary text; Lukács's own critical reconstruction of Hegel's ontology as propaedeutic to a materialist social ontology.
  },
